competition for several national security and commercial launches that had been

00:03:43.861 --> 00:03:48.701
assigned to the vehicle. A reasonable concern while investigators have not yet named the

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:53.641
faulty hardware. A clear finding on the booster engines or the propellant system is what

00:03:53.641 --> 00:03:59.512
would put a timeline on those manifests. Clustered Raptors beat a giant single chamber engine

00:03:59.512 --> 00:04:04.832
from an engineering standpoint because combustion instability grows with the size of the combustor.

00:04:05.392 --> 00:04:09.692
The F1 remains the most powerful single chamber liquid rocket motor ever flown,

00:04:09.692 --> 00:04:16.692
producing 6.77 MN of sea level thrust, and combustion instability during its development forced

00:04:16.692 --> 00:04:21.772
extensive redesigns and testing that ultimately limited further scaling of single chamber designs.

00:04:22.472 --> 00:04:26.732
A large combustor supports low frequency acoustic modes that couple with the burn.

00:04:27.272 --> 00:04:31.972
If a pressure wave hits the injector just as fresh propellant arrives, the burn spikes,

00:04:32.292 --> 00:04:37.452
the wave grows, and the engine can tear itself apart, which is why F1 engineers spent

00:04:37.452 --> 00:04:41.152
years on baffles and injectors instead of simply making the chamber bigger.

00:04:41.952 --> 00:04:46.492
Raptor avoids that trap by using smaller chambers that are easier to stabilize, throttle,

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:48.132
and replace after a flight.

00:04:48.832 --> 00:04:55.192
Sea level variants produce between 230 and 250 tons of thrust, and vacuum-optimized versions

00:04:55.192 --> 00:04:58.332
reach 258 to 275 tons.

00:04:58.952 --> 00:05:04.752
Clustering 33 of those engines on the Super Heavy Booster already exceeds 80 Mn of total thrust.

00:05:04.752 --> 00:05:13.392
33 times 250 tons is about 8,250 tons on the order of 80 Mn, while preserving engine

00:05:13.392 --> 00:05:18.892
out capability, the ability to keep flying after one engine fails and rapid reusability.

00:05:19.392 --> 00:05:24.232
Lose one chamber in a cluster and you still have most of your thrust, lose the only chamber

00:05:24.232 --> 00:05:25.232
and the stage is done.

00:05:25.852 --> 00:05:31.192
The approach trades peak single chamber performance for manufacturing simplicity, higher specific

00:05:31.192 --> 00:05:35.992
impulse, more push from each kilogram of propellant, and the ability to iterate hardware

00:05:35.992 --> 00:05:41.412
between flights rather than between programs. Apollo demonstrated one-shot lunar capability

00:05:41.412 --> 00:05:50.572
with 5 F1 engines. Five times 6.77 MN is about 34 MN, and Super Heavy's more than 80 MN is

00:05:50.572 --> 00:05:56.832
more than double Saturn V, while those engines are meant to return for inspection. RD-170

00:05:56.832 --> 00:06:02.092
family engines generate more total thrust than the F1, but require four chambers precisely

00:06:02.092 --> 00:06:06.312
to manage the same combustion instability that plagued early F1 testing.

00:06:07.072 --> 00:06:12.532
That modular sizing lets SpaceX build, test, and fly individual engines at rates impossible

00:06:12.532 --> 00:06:17.872
with a single massive chamber, and today's clustered, reusable architecture supports sustained

00:06:17.872 --> 00:06:20.212
orbital refueling and higher flight rates.

00:06:20.772 --> 00:06:25.332
The raw material cost of the propellant and metal in a single Raptor is a small fraction

00:06:25.332 --> 00:06:30.692
of the finished engine price. So the idiot index, how many times the finished part costs over

00:06:30.692 --> 00:06:36.352
those raw inputs, stays a high multiple until reuse lets the same engines fly again with minimal

